WN.chunk(35,{"n":[[1267,"Explain charging by friction as the transfer of electrons from one material to another, so one object becomes negative and the other equally positive, with total charge conserved",["Explains which object gains electrons when a balloon is rubbed on hair","States that positive charge is not transferred","Explains why the two objects end up with equal and opposite charges"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.5"]],[1268,"Connect ammeters in series and voltmeters in parallel, read analogue and digital meters with suitable ranges, and check for zero errors",["Draws a circuit with an ammeter and a voltmeter correctly placed","Reads an analogue meter scale","Explains why a voltmeter goes in parallel"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.1"]],[1269,"Explain that conventional current is shown flowing from positive to negative while electrons actually drift from negative to positive, and why the convention persists",["Marks conventional current on a circuit diagram","Marks the direction of electron flow","Explains why the two directions differ"],[]],[1270,"Distinguish permanent magnets from induced magnets that become magnetic only in a field, and explain that induced magnetism always attracts and is lost when the field is removed",["Explains why a paper clip sticks to a magnet and to another clip","States that induced magnetism always causes attraction","Compares magnetically hard and soft materials"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.4"]],[1271,"Recognise and draw symbols for resistors, variable resistors, diodes, LEDs, LDRs, thermistors, fuses, ammeters and voltmeters, and build circuits from diagrams",["Draws the symbols for an LDR and a thermistor","Builds a circuit correctly from a diagram","Identifies the components in an unfamiliar diagram"],[]],[1272,"Distinguish direct current from cells and batteries from alternating current from the mains, and state mains frequency and voltage (230 V at 50 Hz in the UK; 120 V at 60 Hz in the US)",["Sketches pd–time graphs for ac and dc","States mains frequency and voltage for their country","Explains what changes direction in ac"],[]],[1273,"Define current as the rate of flow of charge and use Q = I × t to calculate charge in coulombs, current in amperes or time",["Calculates charge passing in a given time","Rearranges Q = It to find current","Converts minutes to seconds before calculating"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.1"]],[1274,"Explain that a diode lets current flow in one direction only, that an LED is a diode that emits light, and why LEDs are more efficient than filament bulbs",["Predicts whether a lamp lights with a diode either way round","Explains what an LED does differently from a filament bulb","Uses diodes to protect a circuit from reversed batteries"],[]],[1275,"Describe the electric field around a charged object, draw field lines for isolated charges, and explain that a charge in the field feels a force that is stronger closer to the object, causing sparks when the field is strong enough",["Draws field lines around a positive and a negative charge","Explains the direction of force on a charge in a field","Explains why sparks jump from strongly charged objects"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.5","ngss-hs:HS-PS3-5"]],[1276,"Explain how electromagnets are used in relays, electric bells, door locks and scrapyard cranes, and why they are useful because they can be switched off",["Explains how a relay lets a small current switch a large one","Describes the action of an electric bell","Explains why an iron core is used"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.4"]],[1277,"Use systematic reasoning and meter readings to locate faults such as open circuits, short circuits and blown components",["Predicts meter readings for an open circuit","Uses a voltmeter to locate a broken connection","Explains why a short circuit is dangerous"],[]],[1278,"Describe how the resistance of a light-dependent resistor falls as light increases and how a thermistor's resistance falls as temperature rises, with uses in automatic lights and thermostats",["States how each component's resistance changes","Sketches resistance against light intensity for an LDR","Suggests a use for each sensor"],[]],[1279,"Identify the live, neutral and earth wires and their functions, and explain how the earth wire and fuse protect people when a fault makes a metal case live",["States the function of each wire in a three-core cable","Explains why touching the live wire is dangerous even when switched off","Explains how earth and fuse work together"],[]],[1280,"Define potential difference as the energy transferred per unit charge (1 V = 1 J/C) and use V = E/Q",["Explains what 12 V means in joules and coulombs","Calculates energy transferred by a charge moving through a pd","Distinguishes pd across a component from current through it"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.1"]],[1281,"Calculate total resistance in series (R = R₁ + R₂ + …), explain that current is the same everywhere and that the supply pd is shared in proportion to resistance",["Calculates total resistance of resistors in series","Finds the current and each pd in a series circuit","Explains why the larger resistor takes the larger share of pd"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.2"]],[1282,"Explain dangers of static (sparks when refuelling, shocks) and how earthing prevents them, and describe uses such as electrostatic spray painting, photocopiers and smoke precipitators",["Explains why fuel tankers are earthed","Describes how electrostatic spray painting gives an even coat","Explains what earthing does to excess charge"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.5"]],[1283,"Investigate how a wire's length, thickness, material and temperature affect its resistance, controlling variables and plotting resistance against length",["Predicts how doubling length changes resistance","Plans a fair test of resistance against length","Explains why thicker wires have lower resistance"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.1"]],[1284,"Explain why appliances in homes are connected in parallel — each gets the full mains pd and can be switched independently — and what happens to total current as more appliances are switched on",["Explains two advantages of parallel wiring at home","Predicts how total current changes as appliances are added","Explains why too many appliances on one socket is unsafe"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.2"]],[1285,"Compare an alternator (slip rings, ac output) with a dynamo (split-ring commutator, dc output) and sketch their output graphs, explaining how speed affects frequency and peak voltage",["Sketches output graphs for each","Explains the role of slip rings and commutator","Predicts the effect of turning faster"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.3"]],[1286,"Calculate the normal current of an appliance from I = P/V and choose a suitable fuse rating, and compare fuses, circuit breakers and residual current devices",["Calculates the current for a 2 kW kettle on 230 V","Chooses the right fuse from 3 A, 5 A and 13 A","Explains an advantage of a circuit breaker"],[]],[1287,"Use Fleming's left-hand rule to find the direction of the force on a wire carrying current in a magnetic field, and calculate it with F = BIL",["Uses the left-hand rule to predict force direction","Calculates force from field strength, current and length","Explains why there is no force when the wire is parallel to the field"],[]],[1288,"Explain how forces on opposite sides of a coil make it turn, why a split-ring commutator is needed to keep it turning, and how to make the motor faster",["Explains why the forces on opposite sides form a turning effect","Explains the job of the split-ring commutator","Lists ways to increase the speed of a motor"],[]],[1289,"Describe the National Grid as a network linking power stations to consumers, with step-up transformers to very high voltages for transmission and step-down transformers for safe use",["Draws a labelled block diagram of the grid","States where step-up and step-down transformers are used","Explains why homes are not supplied at transmission voltage"],[]],[1290,"Explain how an alternating current in the primary coil creates a changing magnetic field in an iron core that induces an alternating pd in the secondary, and why transformers do not work with dc",["Labels primary coil, secondary coil and core","Explains why a changing field is needed","Distinguishes step-up from step-down transformers"],[]],[1291,"Obtain and interpret current–voltage graphs for a fixed resistor (straight line through origin), a filament lamp (curve) and a diode (current one way only), explaining each shape",["Sketches the I–V graph for each component","Explains why the lamp's graph curves","Identifies an ohmic conductor from its graph"],[]],[1292,"Explain that a loudspeaker uses the motor effect to vibrate a cone with a varying current, and that a moving-coil microphone uses induction to turn sound vibrations into a varying current",["Explains how a varying current moves a speaker cone","Explains how a microphone produces a signal","Compares the two as reverse processes"],[]],[1293,"Explain resistance in metals as electrons colliding with vibrating metal ions, and use this to explain why resistance rises with temperature and why current heats a wire",["Describes electrons colliding with lattice ions","Explains why a filament's resistance rises as it heats","Links collisions to the heating effect of a current"],[]],[1294,"Explain why adding resistors in parallel decreases total resistance (more paths), show the total is less than the smallest resistor, and calculate it with 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂",["Explains why adding a branch increases total current","Calculates total resistance of two resistors in parallel","Checks that the answer is smaller than either resistor"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.2"]],[1295,"Combine Ohm's law with series and parallel rules to find unknown currents, potential differences and resistances in mixed circuits",["Finds every current and pd in a circuit with a parallel pair in series with a resistor","Sets out working in clear steps","Checks answers using the rules for pd and current"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.ElectricityAndElectromagnetism.2"]],[1296,"Use Vp/Vs = Np/Ns and, for an ideal transformer, Vp × Ip = Vs × Is to calculate voltages, currents and numbers of turns",["Calculates secondary voltage from turns ratio","Finds secondary current using power conservation","Designs a transformer for a given output"],[]],[1297,"Explain how grid operators meet changing demand through the day and year, the challenge of variable renewables, and the role of storage such as pumped hydro and batteries",["Describes a daily demand curve","Explains why wind and solar complicate supply","Evaluates a storage option"],[]],[1298,"Compare power lost in transmission cables at different voltages using P = I²R and P = IV, showing quantitatively why high-voltage transmission cuts losses",["Calculates current in cables for a given power at two voltages","Calculates power lost in each case","Explains the result in terms of current squared"],[]],[1299,"Describe and graph the exponential decay of charge, current and pd as a capacitor discharges through a resistor, and interpret the time constant RC",["Sketches charge against time during discharge","Explains why the current falls more slowly over time","Uses the time constant to estimate discharge time"],[]],[1300,"Explain that a capacitor stores energy by separating equal and opposite charges on two plates (the charge Q on each plate), use Q = CV, and describe uses such as camera flashes and smoothing",["Calculates charge stored for a given capacitance and pd","Describes what happens in a capacitor as it charges","Gives a use of a capacitor"],[]],[1301,"Explain that an induced current always flows in a direction that opposes the change producing it, and use this to predict poles and current directions",["Predicts the pole formed when a magnet approaches a coil","Explains why moving a magnet into a coil takes effort","Links Lenz's law to conservation of energy"],[]],[1302,"Define electric field strength as force per unit charge, calculate it with E = F/Q, and for parallel plates use E = V/d to describe a uniform field",["Calculates the force on a charge in a known field","Calculates the field between parallel plates","Draws the uniform field between plates"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS3-5"]],[1303,"Distinguish emf from terminal pd, explain internal resistance as energy lost inside the source, and use ε = I(R + r) to analyse cells",["Explains why terminal pd drops when more current is drawn","Calculates internal resistance from measurements","Plots terminal pd against current and reads emf and r"],[]],[1304,"State that the induced emf equals the rate of change of magnetic flux linkage, and use it to explain why faster motion, stronger fields and more turns give larger emfs",["Explains induced emf in terms of rate of change","Calculates an average induced emf from a change in flux","Links the law to generator design"],[]],[1305,"Apply conservation of charge at junctions (currents in = currents out) and conservation of energy round any loop (sum of emfs = sum of pds) to analyse circuits",["Uses the junction rule to find an unknown current","Uses the loop rule to find an unknown pd","Links each law to a conservation principle"],[]],[1306,"Explain how two resistors in series divide the supply pd in the ratio of their resistances, calculate output voltages, and use an LDR or thermistor to make a sensing circuit",["Calculates the output pd of a potential divider","Predicts how the output changes when an LDR is covered","Designs a divider that gives a set output"],[]],[1307,"Use R = ρL/A to calculate resistance from resistivity, length and cross-sectional area, and compare materials",["Calculates resistance of a wire of given dimensions","Finds resistivity from experimental data","Explains why resistance halves when area doubles"],[]],[1308,"Explain how solar cells release electrons when they absorb light of high enough frequency, producing a pd, and evaluate solar power output and limitations",["Describes the energy transfer in a solar cell","Explains why output depends on light intensity","Evaluates solar panels for a location"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS4-5"]]],"r":[[2474,"KS3 static charge from rubbing is explained by electrons moving from one material to another"],[2475,"Only electrons move when charging by friction, because protons are fixed in the nucleus"],[2476,"An electric field surrounds any charged object, such as one charged by friction"],[2477,"Magnetic field lines around a magnet give the model for drawing electric field lines"],[2478,"Sparks when refuelling and earthing are explained by electrons building up and flowing away"],[2479,"Sparks jump where the electric field is strong enough to make the air conduct"],[2480,"The KS3 idea of current as a flow of charge is made quantitative with Q = It"],[2481,"In metal wires the moving charge is carried by free electrons drifting through the metal"],[2482,"Finding charge, current or time means substituting into Q = It"],[2483,"The direction shown for current on a circuit diagram is the convention being explained"],[2484,"Electrons are negative, so they drift from negative to positive, against conventional current"],[2485,"V = E/Q needs the charge in coulombs, found from current and time"],[2486,"The KS3 picture of voltage as a push is replaced by energy transferred per coulomb"],[2487,"Potential difference measures the energy transferred electrically between stores"],[2488,"Using meters correctly needs to know that ammeters measure current and voltmeters pd"],[2489,"Placing meters in series or parallel is planned on a circuit drawn with standard symbols"],[2490,"Reading scales accurately and choosing suitable ranges are general measurement skills"],[2491,"Primary circuit symbols for cells, bulbs and switches are extended to resistors and sensors"],[2492,"Each resistance in the investigation is calculated from meter readings with R = V/I"],[2493,"Testing length means keeping the material, thickness and temperature the same"],[2494,"A straight line through the origin shows that resistance is proportional to length"],[2495,"The particle model explains why longer, thinner and hotter wires have more resistance"],[2496,"Free electrons moving between a lattice of metal ions is the model of metallic structure used"],[2497,"R = ρL/A puts numbers to the measured effects of length and cross-sectional area"],[2498,"Doubling the cross-sectional area halves the resistance, an inverse proportion"],[2499,"Resistivities such as 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ Ω m are handled in standard form"],[2500,"Each graph tests whether V = IR holds with a constant resistance"],[2501,"The data come from ammeter and voltmeter readings as the pd is varied"],[2502,"A straight line through the origin means constant resistance; a curve means it changes"],[2503,"The lamp's curve is explained by the filament heating up and its resistance rising"],[2504,"Using LDRs and thermistors in circuits needs their symbols and how they are connected"],[2505,"A falling resistance means a larger current for the same pd, by V = IR"],[2506,"The diode symbol's arrow shows the one direction in which it lets current flow"],[2507,"The arrow on the diode symbol points the way conventional current flows"],[2508,"LEDs are compared with filament bulbs by efficiency, the useful light output per joule"],[2509,"KS3 rules that series current is the same everywhere are now used to share the pd"],[2510,"The pd across each resistor is found from V = IR with the common current"],[2511,"KS3 parallel rules, with the same pd across each branch, explain why resistance falls"],[2512,"Parallel resistances are contrasted with series ones, which simply add"],[2513,"Using 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ means adding fractions and then taking the reciprocal"],[2514,"In series sections, resistances add and the pd is shared in proportion to resistance"],[2515,"In parallel sections, branches share the same pd and the combined resistance is found from reciprocals"],[2516,"An unknown resistance or current is often found by writing and solving a linear equation"],[2517,"The junction and loop rules generalise the series and parallel rules used in mixed circuits"],[2518,"The loop rule, emfs equal the sum of pds, is conservation of energy for each coulomb"],[2519,"A potential divider is two resistors in series sharing the supply pd in the ratio of resistances"],[2520,"Sensing circuits replace one resistor with an LDR or thermistor so the output changes with conditions"],[2521,"Working out each output voltage is sharing the supply pd in a given ratio"],[2522,"Emf and terminal pd are both energy per coulomb, given to or used by the charge"],[2523,"The cell's internal resistance is treated as a series resistor in the circuit"],[2524,"A graph of terminal pd against current is a straight line with intercept ε and gradient −r"],[2525,"Fault-finding compares ammeter and voltmeter readings with the values expected"],[2526,"Spotting a fault needs the normal current and pd pattern for series and parallel parts"],[2527,"The charge Q stored on each plate is measured in coulombs, as in Q = It"],[2528,"The charge stored is proportional to the pd across the plates, Q = CV"],[2529,"A charged capacitor stores energy in the electric field between its plates"],[2530,"Discharge describes the stored charge Q = CV flowing away through a resistor"],[2531,"Charge, current and pd each fall by the same factor in equal times, an exponential decay"],[2532,"Telling ac from dc means tracking how the current and pd change direction over time"],[2533,"Mains frequency, 50 Hz in the UK, is the number of complete cycles each second"],[2534,"The live wire carries the alternating pd of the mains supply, with neutral near 0 V"],[2535,"Plastic casings and cable insulation protect users because they are electrical insulators"],[2536,"A fuse in the live wire works with the earth wire to cut the supply when a fault occurs"],[2537,"The appliance's normal current is found from P = IV before choosing the fuse rating"],[2538,"An induced magnet only becomes magnetic while it sits in another magnet's field"],[2539,"Only magnetic materials such as iron, nickel and cobalt can become induced magnets"],[2540,"Relays, bells and locks use a KS3 electromagnet that can be switched on and off"],[2541,"Relay and bell circuits are drawn with standard symbols for switches and coils"],[2542,"The KS3 motor effect is given a direction rule and a formula, F = BIL"],[2543,"The force arises because the wire's own magnetic field interacts with the magnet's field"],[2544,"Calculating the force means substituting field, current and length into F = BIL"],[2545,"Opposite sides of the coil carry current in opposite directions, so the forces turn it"],[2546,"The forces on the coil sides produce a turning effect, or moment, about the axle"],[2547,"A varying current in the speaker coil gives a varying force that vibrates the cone"],[2548,"A microphone's coil moves in a magnetic field, inducing a current that copies the sound"],[2549,"The vibrating cone pushes the air into compressions and rarefactions, which is sound"],[2550,"Lenz's law gives the direction of the current produced by induction in a generator"],[2551,"If the induced current helped the change, energy would be created, breaking conservation"],[2552,"Faraday's law gives the size of the induced emf, while Lenz's law gives its direction"],[2553,"The emf equals the rate of change of flux linkage, the gradient of a flux–time graph"],[2554,"Both machines produce current by rotating a coil in a magnetic field"],[2555,"An alternator gives ac and a dynamo gives dc, so their output graphs are compared"],[2556,"The split-ring commutator in a dynamo works as it does in a dc motor"],[2557,"The changing field in the core induces a pd in the secondary coil by electromagnetic induction"],[2558,"Only an alternating current makes the continuously changing field that a transformer needs"],[2559,"The equations describe how the turns on each coil set the step-up or step-down in pd"],[2560,"Vp/Vs = Np/Ns says the voltages are in the same ratio as the numbers of turns"],[2561,"For an ideal transformer, power in equals power out, so Vp × Ip = Vs × Is"],[2562,"Step-up and step-down transformers change the voltage between power station and home"],[2563,"Power stations supply the electricity that the grid carries to consumers"],[2564,"The calculation shows numerically why the grid steps the voltage up for transmission"],[2565,"Stepping the voltage up steps the current down by the same factor, for the same power"],[2566,"Power lost in the cables is I²R, so reducing the current cuts losses sharply"],[2567,"Balancing supply and demand is the day-to-day work of running the grid"],[2568,"Which stations can start quickly or vary output depends on the energy resource each uses"],[2569,"Light of high enough frequency releases electrons, the photon idea behind solar cells"],[2570,"A solar cell's output is described as the pd it produces, the energy given to each coulomb"],[2571,"E = F/Q puts a number to the field drawn qualitatively with field lines"],[2572,"Between parallel plates, E = V/d links the field strength to the pd across them"],[2573,"Charges such as 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and large field strengths are handled in standard form"],[2574,"In parallel, each branch gets the full supply pd and can be switched on its own"],[2575,"Household circuits run on the mains ac supply, so each appliance gets the full 230 V"],[2576,"Adding appliances raises the total current, which is why fuses protect circuits"]],"c":{"Science|Electricity & Circuits|12":"Your child is beginning to study electricity in more depth — explaining static charge as electrons moving between objects, and measuring current and voltage with ammeters and voltmeters.","Science|Electricity & Circuits|13":"Your child is learning the conventions of circuits and magnets — the difference between conventional current and electron flow, standard symbols for circuit components, and how permanent and induced magnets differ.","Science|Electricity & Circuits|14":"Your child is learning how circuits work quantitatively — current as flow of charge, voltage as energy per charge, how components such as diodes and thermistors behave, and how homes are wired safely.","Science|Electricity & Circuits|15":"Your child is learning how electricity and magnetism work together — solving multi-step circuit problems and explaining how motors, loudspeakers, generators, transformers and the National Grid work.","Science|Electricity & Circuits|16":"Your child is studying advanced electricity — resistivity, Kirchhoff's laws, potential dividers, internal resistance, capacitors, the laws of electromagnetic induction, and how the grid keeps supply matched to demand."}});
